Adding here a summary of the analysis so we don’t start from scratch next time:

* The [+Bulk provider portal creation+|https://admin-panel.bolt.eu/delivery/providerPortal/bulkAccountCreate] page can indeed be opened when the link is sent directly, even if the user does not have permission. However, when trying to upload a CSV, this action is denied due to missing permission. So even if the UI is a bit confusing, functionality is as expected
* Curier team implemented their own method to overcome this behavior but it seems a bit hackish.
* We seem to have the appropriate permissions validation on:
** the frontend side in [manifest.json|https://github.com/bolteu/admin-panel/blob/develop/modules/delivery-provider/manifest.json] 
{code:xml}{"title": "Provider Portal","children": [{"title": "Bulk account creation","link": "/providerPortal/bulkAccountCreate","permission": {"type": "oneOf","values": ["delivery_provider_portal_accounts_bulk_create"]}}],"permission": {"type": "oneOf","values": ["delivery_provider_portal_accounts_bulk_create"]}},{code}
** and on the backend side in the [AdminPanelController|https://github.com/bolteu/bolt-server/blob/develop/src/delivery/provider/delivery_provider/main/controller/AdminPanelController.ts]
** 
{noformat}    public createProviderPortalAccountsInBulk = async (
        ctx: Context,
        auth: AdminPanelAuthStrategy.AuthResponse,
        _query: AdminPanelApp.StandardRequest,
        request: CreateProviderPortalAccountsInBulkRequest
    ): Promise<ResponseObject<CreateProviderPortalAccountsInBulkResponse>> => {
        AuthPermissions.assert(ctx, auth, Permission.DELIVERY_PROVIDER_PORTAL_ACCOUNTS_BULK_CREATE);
        const result = await this.adminPanelService.createProviderPortalAccountsFromCsv(ctx, request.csv_file_data);
        return Response.data(result);
    };{noformat}
* To properly solve this, one should start a discussion on slack #dev-admin-panel-support → check with the team how to check permissions in a form, without calling the backend

Hi [~accountid:6078612fb5dffc006f5413c9] 

Hi Oualid,

Following our conversation on the 3PL’s mechanics, please see below a summary of what you can and cannot do in terms of managing them:

+*You can*+ give instructions to the 3PL's employees in relation to: 

* Health & safety;
* Instructions and training on the safe use of the infrastructure, operation of machines, equipment, products and handling of materials and the execution of the Services in compliance with Bolt's policies and procedures in relation to (i) data protection & privacy (ii) compliance, code of conduct; and (iii) security and health safety at work obligations;
* Instructions in relation to the modalities of the Services, such as general guidelines and information to ensure the proper performance and delivery of the Services, and for the efficient collaboration between the 3PL and Bolt;
* Instructions related to protection of intellectual property and documenting and storage of the results, such as in notebooks, reports and databases.

If Bolt is required to give other instructions to the 3PL's employees, Bolt should contact the 3PL Contractor in this respect in writing in order to get the right to give new instructions, provided that such instructions do not have any impact on the 3PL employer's authority over its employees.

+*You cannot:*+

* Recruit 3PL's mechanics (process, interviews, selection criteria and recruitment, etc.);
* Give compensation and benefits (definition and payment of salaries, bonuses, benefits and reimbursements of expenses, etc.);
* Conduct career management and assessment of performance and competence;
* Subscribe insurance policies for the 3PL mechanics;
* Monitor attendances and absences (monitoring of working time, authorisation and justification of absences);
* Offer training or personal development, except if these are specific and necessary for the performance of the Services;
* Get involved with disciplinary sanctions & associated policies or dismissal & associated policies;
* Interfere with absence from work (holidays, sick leaves & other).

In short, *Bolt shall not be entitled to give any instructions to the 3PL's employees in relation to holidays, salary, individual performance evaluation, nor to take any disciplinary actions towards the 3PL’s employees.*


Please note that employee lending is prohibited. In order to avoid such an issue and to limit the risk – which is sanctioned by civil and criminal sanctions – , the external counsel has revised article 2 and article 10 of the Agreement and has inserted an instruction right clause, which sets out the limited 'instructions' Bolt can give to the Contractors' employees without caving out the Contractor's employers authority over these employees. 

If Bolt does not exercise any employer's authority over the Contractor's employees and limits its instructions to those included in the template, chances of employees lending are basically non-existing. 

FYI prohibited employee lending triggers the following potential sanctions: 

* Civil law sanctions: the employee(s) will be deemed to be employed by Bolt on the basis of an employment contract of undefined duration and Bolt would also be jointly liable for all salary, social security and taxes. 
* Criminal law sanctions: A level 3 sanction of the Social Criminal Code would apply, i.e. either an administrative sanction ranging between 400 and 4,000 EUR or a criminal fine ranging between 800 and 8,000 EUR, to be multiplied with the number of employees involved (capped at 100). 

 

From our conversation earlier this week, we understand that you are already following the advice provided above.

Should you have any further questions, please do let us know.

Many thanks.

